T09.01|Common-size statements express each item as a share of a selected base. Income-statement items often use revenue; balance-sheet items often use total assets. This removes absolute scale and helps compare cost structure or financing composition across firms and periods.~~The base must be economically meaningful and consistently defined. A margin can improve while absolute profit falls if revenue shrinks. Common-size analysis complements, rather than replaces, currency amounts and cash-flow analysis.|Income-statement common size: Each item divided by revenue.;;Balance-sheet common size: Each item divided by total assets or another stated base.;;Component analysis: Shares within a selected category.|Company A has ₹150 lakh revenue and ₹30 operating profit; B has ₹500 revenue and ₹75 operating profit.~~A's operating margin = 30/150 = 20%.;;B's operating margin = 75/500 = 15%.;;B earns more absolute profit, while A earns more operating profit per revenue rupee.~~Size leadership and margin leadership differ.~~Business mix, accounting policies and asset requirements can explain the gap; margin alone does not establish better investment value.|State the denominator.;;Retain absolute amounts alongside percentages.;;Investigate business and accounting differences.
T09.02|Horizontal analysis examines change through time. Vertical analysis examines a statement's composition within a period. Together they show both what grew and how the business structure changed. Percentage growth from a tiny or negative starting value can be misleading.~~An expense growing slower than revenue can improve margin even when its currency amount rises. Comparisons require consistent classifications, period lengths and consolidation scope.|Absolute horizontal change: Current minus prior amount.;;Relative horizontal change: Change divided by prior amount.;;Vertical analysis: Item divided by a within-period base.|Revenue grows from ₹100 to ₹120 lakh; selling expense grows from ₹20 to ₹22.~~Revenue increases 20%; selling expense increases 10%.;;Expense share falls from 20/100 = 20% to 22/120 = 18.33%.;;The firm spends ₹2 more but uses less selling expense per sales rupee.~~An absolute cost increase can coexist with improved cost efficiency.~~Changes in business mix or expense classification need investigation before attributing the change to management execution.|Check comparability first.;;Calculate amounts and percentages.;;Explain composition changes alongside growth.
T09.03|Growth analysis measures change in revenue, earnings, assets or cash over time. Trend analysis asks whether the pattern is persistent and economically explainable. Organic growth should be separated from acquisitions, currency translation and accounting changes where information permits.~~Growth can consume capital and cash. An increasing sales figure creates value only if margins, reinvestment and risk support returns above the relevant cost of capital.|Year-on-year growth: Comparison with the same prior-year period.;;Compound growth: Endpoint annualised trend.;;Organic versus acquired growth: Existing operations separated from additions.|Revenue rises from ₹100 to ₹130 lakh, including ₹20 contributed by an acquired business. Assume no other scope or currency changes.~~Reported growth = 30%.;;Comparable existing-operation revenue is ₹110.;;Simplified organic growth is (110 − 100)/100 = 10%.~~Most reported growth comes from changed business scope in this illustration.~~A complete analysis also considers acquisition cost, acquired margins and the timing of consolidation.|Reconcile scope and currency.;;Review several periods and drivers.;;Connect growth to incremental capital and cash.
T09.04|Liquidity ratios compare near-term obligations with assets expected to be available. Current ratio includes all current assets; quick ratio excludes less immediately liquid items according to a stated definition; cash ratio focuses on cash and qualifying near-cash resources. Definitions vary, so disclose inclusions.~~A high ratio is not a guarantee of payment capacity. Old inventory, disputed receivables, restricted deposits and seasonal timing can make the apparent buffer less usable.|Current ratio: Current assets/current liabilities.;;Quick ratio: Selected liquid current assets/current liabilities.;;Cash ratio: Selected cash resources/current liabilities.|Current assets are cash ₹10 lakh, receivables ₹30 and inventory ₹40; current liabilities are ₹50. Assume no other items.~~Current ratio = 80/50 = 1.6.;;Quick ratio excluding inventory = 40/50 = 0.8.;;Cash ratio = 10/50 = 0.2.~~Liquidity looks different as asset eligibility narrows.~~Collection timing and maturity deadlines determine whether the receivables bridge can actually cover the obligations.|State ratio definitions.;;Test asset recoverability and restrictions.;;Use a dated cash forecast with the ratios.
T09.05|Margins measure earnings per unit of revenue at different stages. Gross margin reflects revenue after direct cost; operating margin includes operating expenses; net margin includes financing, tax and other recognised effects. A margin change should be traced to prices, volume, mix, costs and accounting.~~A high net margin driven by an asset-sale gain may not represent sustainable operations. Compare recurring margins and cash economics while clearly reconciling adjustments.|Gross margin: Gross profit/revenue.;;Operating margin: Defined operating profit/revenue.;;Net margin: Defined net profit/revenue.|Revenue is ₹150 lakh, cost of sales ₹90, other operating expense ₹30, and net profit ₹15.~~Gross margin = (150 − 90)/150 = 40%.;;Operating margin = (150 − 90 − 30)/150 = 20%.;;Net margin = 15/150 = 10%.~~Each margin reflects a different layer of costs and claims.~~Definitions of operating earnings must be consistent across companies; unexplained adjusted margins can conceal exclusions.|Reconcile the profit layers.;;Separate recurring and unusual effects.;;Analyse price, volume, mix and cost drivers.
T09.06|Turnover ratios relate period activity to resources employed. Asset turnover measures sales per asset rupee; inventory turnover and receivable days analyse operating-cycle efficiency. Average balances often better match a full-year flow than a single closing balance.~~Higher turnover can reflect efficiency, outsourcing or simply a different business model. Extremely low inventory may increase stockouts; rapid receivable turnover may reflect factoring rather than improved customer payment.|Asset turnover: Revenue/average asset base.;;Inventory turnover: Appropriate cost flow/average inventory.;;Receivable days: Average eligible receivables/credit sales × days.|Annual cost of sales is ₹360 lakh, average inventory ₹60, and the year has 365 days.~~Inventory turnover = 360/60 = 6 times.;;Inventory days = 60/360 × 365 ≈ 60.83 days.;;Check monthly averages if year-end inventory is unusually low.~~Turnover and days express the same cycle in different units under matching definitions.~~Use cost of sales, not revenue, when comparing inventory measured at cost.|Match flow and stock definitions.;;Use representative averages.;;Investigate operating trade-offs and financing transactions.
T09.07|Leverage ratios describe debt relative to capital or earnings; coverage ratios compare earnings or cash with required payments; solvency analysis assesses whether obligations remain sustainable. Debt/EBITDA is a burden indicator, while interest coverage addresses one part of servicing. Neither alone captures principal maturities.~~Definitions may include leases, guarantees or pension obligations. EBITDA is not cash available to lenders after tax, reinvestment and working-capital needs.|Debt/capital: Funding composition.;;Debt/EBITDA: Debt burden relative to a defined earnings measure.;;Interest coverage: Earnings relative to interest.;;Debt-service coverage: Cash available relative to interest plus principal under a stated definition.|Debt is ₹120 lakh, EBITDA ₹40, EBIT ₹30, interest ₹5 and principal due ₹20.~~Debt/EBITDA = 3 times.;;EBIT/interest = 6 times.;;Interest plus principal due is ₹25, requiring a separate cash-service assessment.~~Strong interest coverage does not demonstrate sufficient cash for principal repayment.~~Covenant definitions may differ from analytical definitions and must be read directly.|Specify debt and earnings inclusions.;;Examine maturity and covenant schedules.;;Stress cash available for total service.
T09.08|ROA, ROE, ROCE and ROIC match different profit measures to different resource bases. ROE uses profit attributable to equity relative to equity; an operating ROIC formulation uses NOPAT relative to invested operating capital. ROCE definitions vary and must be stated.~~Leverage can raise ROE without improving operating returns. Compare a return with a cost of capital covering the same claimants; ROIC versus WACC is a common operating comparison.|ROA: Defined earnings relative to average assets.;;ROE: Equity earnings relative to average equity.;;ROCE: Defined operating earnings relative to capital employed.;;ROIC: NOPAT relative to average operating invested capital.|Assume NOPAT ₹15 lakh, invested capital ₹100, net income ₹10 and average equity ₹50.~~ROIC = 15/100 = 15%.;;ROE = 10/50 = 20%.;;At WACC 10%, operating economic profit is 15 − 100 × 10% = ₹5.~~Different returns can be correct because their numerators and capital bases differ.~~Use average balances and explain adjustments; do not compare ROE directly with WACC.|Match profit to capital ownership.;;State operating and non-operating adjustments.;;Compare with the corresponding required return.
T09.09|DuPont analysis decomposes ROE into drivers. The three-part version is net margin × asset turnover × equity multiplier, using compatible average balances. It separates earnings per sales rupee, sales per asset rupee and financial leverage.~~An improving ROE can result from better operations or a smaller equity base and greater leverage. The decomposition diagnoses the source; it does not automatically judge whether the change is sustainable or desirable.|Three-part decomposition: Margin, turnover and leverage.;;Extended decomposition: Additional tax and interest-burden layers.;;Operating versus funding drivers: Business performance separated from balance-sheet leverage.|Net income is ₹10 lakh, revenue ₹100, average assets ₹50 and average equity ₹25.~~Net margin = 10%; asset turnover = 100/50 = 2.;;Equity multiplier = 50/25 = 2.;;ROE = 10% × 2 × 2 = 40%, also 10/25.~~The product reconciles to directly calculated ROE.~~A negative or very small equity base can make the ratio unstable or uninformative.|Use identical periods and averages.;;Reconcile the product to direct ROE.;;Explain whether change came from operations or leverage.
T09.10|Cash-conversion analysis asks how recognised earnings become cash. Operating cash flow differs from profit through non-cash items and operating working-capital movements. Ratios such as CFO/net income need consistent definitions and multi-period interpretation.~~One year's weak conversion may reflect sound growth investment, seasonal timing or deteriorating collections. Strong conversion can result from temporary supplier stretching, customer advances or shrinking operations.|Earnings-to-cash conversion: Operating cash relative to recognised profit.;;Working-capital bridge: Receivables, inventory and payables movements.;;Free-cash conversion: Cash after defined capital investment.|Profit is ₹20 lakh, depreciation ₹5 and operating working capital increases ₹12. Assume no other adjustments.~~Add back non-cash depreciation: ₹20 + ₹5 = ₹25.;;Subtract the working-capital investment: ₹25 − ₹12 = ₹13 CFO.;;CFO/net income = 13/20 = 65%.~~Only ₹13 of operating cash accompanies ₹20 of earnings under the assumptions.~~Depreciation add-back does not remove the eventual need to maintain productive assets.|Reconcile all adjustments.;;Inspect several periods and seasonality.;;Separate sustainable conversion from timing benefits.
T09.11|Operating leverage arises from fixed operating costs; financial leverage arises from funding with fixed financing obligations. Both can amplify changes in equity earnings. The effect becomes especially large near break-even or when earnings barely cover interest.~~A useful simplified model separates price, variable cost, fixed operating cost and interest. Real businesses have mixed costs, capacity steps and tax effects, so a constant leverage ratio is only a local approximation.|Operating leverage: Fixed costs amplify operating-profit changes.;;Financial leverage: Fixed financing costs amplify equity-profit changes.;;Combined leverage: Both mechanisms act together.|Revenue is ₹100 lakh, variable costs 60%, fixed operating costs ₹30 and interest ₹5. Ignore tax.~~Base EBIT = 100 − 60 − 30 = ₹10; earnings after interest = ₹5.;;At revenue ₹110, EBIT = 110 − 66 − 30 = ₹14.;;After-interest earnings rise to ₹9: 10% sales growth produces 80% earnings growth.~~Fixed costs and interest amplify the change.~~The amplification also works downward; it is exposure, not a free improvement in business quality.|Separate fixed and variable costs.;;Model interest and principal obligations.;;Test downside near break-even.
T09.12|Sustainable-growth models relate growth to profitability, retention and funding policy under specified assumptions. A common simplified formula is growth = ROE × retention when ratios and financing assumptions remain stable. Internal growth considers expansion without external financing under its chosen convention.~~Formula variants depend on whether opening or closing equity and assets are used. These rates are modelling relationships, not guarantees that customers, capacity and lenders will support the resulting growth.|Retention ratio: Earnings retained after distributions.;;Sustainable growth: Growth compatible with an assumed leverage and funding policy.;;Internal growth: Growth supported without specified external funding.|Assume stable ROE 15%, payout 40%, and use the simplified ROE × retention convention.~~Retention = 1 − 40% = 60%.;;Modelled sustainable growth = 15% × 60% = 9%.;;A 20% sales-growth plan may require changed returns, retention, leverage or new equity.~~The funding model highlights a growth-financing gap.~~Check capital intensity and working capital before treating 9% as a universal ceiling.|State the formula convention.;;Check stable-ratio assumptions.;;Link planned growth to actual cash and financing needs.
T09.13|Incremental return on capital evaluates new capital rather than the average historical business. It relates additional operating profit after tax to additional invested capital under a stated timing convention. Attractive legacy assets can mask poor new investment.~~Match the profit increase with the investment that generated it, allowing for ramp-up, acquisitions and cyclical effects. A one-year ratio can be distorted when the capital is committed before earnings mature.|Average ROIC: Return on the existing capital base.;;Incremental ROIC: Additional profit relative to additional capital.;;Project return: Cash-flow valuation of a specific investment.|A business previously earned ₹30 lakh NOPAT on ₹100 capital. It invests another ₹50 and later earns ₹35.~~Original ROIC = 30%.;;Incremental return = (35 − 30)/50 = 10%.;;At a 12% required operating return, the added mature investment underperforms the hurdle.~~Strong average historical returns can coexist with weak new investment.~~Ramp-up and external changes must be considered before attributing the entire profit change to the new capital.|Match investment and resulting earnings.;;Normalise cycle and acquisition effects.;;Compare mature incremental returns with a compatible hurdle.
T09.14|Segment analysis separates business activities whose economics may differ. Consolidated growth can hide a shrinking high-margin segment offset by expansion in a low-margin segment. Segment results need reconciliation with corporate costs, eliminations and reporting policies.~~Revenue mix, profit mix, capital intensity and cash generation are all relevant. Management's segment metric may exclude costs that remain real at group level.|Revenue mix: Contribution to group sales.;;Profit mix: Contribution to a defined earnings measure.;;Capital and cash analysis: Resources and funding tied to each segment.|Segment A has revenue ₹100 lakh and profit ₹20; B has revenue ₹200 and profit ₹10. Corporate cost is ₹5.~~A margin = 20%; B margin = 5%.;;Combined segment profit = ₹30.;;Group profit after corporate cost is ₹25, assuming no other reconciliation items.~~The larger sales segment is not the larger profit contributor.~~Allocations, transfer prices and unreported capital data can limit direct segment comparisons.|Read segment metric definitions.;;Reconcile to group accounts.;;Assess mix, capital needs and common costs.
T09.15|Peer benchmarking compares firms with sufficiently similar economics, not merely the same industry label. Growth, geography, customer mix, maturity, capital intensity and reporting policy can all explain ratio differences. Industry-specific measures address business models poorly represented by generic ratios.~~For example, bank analysis focuses on spread, credit quality, funding and capital; industrial cash cycles focus on inventory and receivables. A generic EBITDA comparison may obscure rather than clarify financial-sector economics.|Operating peers: Similar activities and cost structures.;;Valuation peers: Similar growth, risk and capital economics.;;Industry metrics: Measures tailored to business mechanics.|A retailer reports 8% operating margin and a software business 25%.~~Identify differences in gross margin, recurring revenue and asset requirements.;;Compare each with relevant operating peers first.;;Then assess returns and valuation using consistent risk and capital assumptions.~~A cross-industry margin ranking does not establish investment superiority.~~Even close peers need accounting and cycle adjustments.|Define the peer-selection logic.;;Normalise metrics and periods.;;Explain differences rather than treating the median as a target.
T09.16|Normalisation adjusts reported statements to estimate comparable ongoing economics. Potential items include unusual gains, temporary disruption, accounting-policy differences and non-operating assets. Adjustments must be evidenced and reconciled; recurring costs should not disappear simply because management labels them exceptional.~~Normalisation is an analytical estimate, not a replacement for reported results. Use consistent treatment of both favourable and unfavourable items and assess related cash, tax and balance-sheet effects.|Earnings adjustment: Remove or restate a justified unusual item.;;Balance-sheet adjustment: Align asset or liability definitions.;;Cycle normalisation: Estimate representative mid-cycle activity.|Reported operating profit is ₹30 lakh, including an asset-sale gain ₹8 and a genuinely isolated disruption cost ₹3.~~Remove the gain: ₹30 − ₹8 = ₹22.;;Add back the supported disruption cost: ₹22 + ₹3 = ₹25.;;Reconcile reported ₹30 to normalised ₹25 and explain each item.~~Normalised profit is lower despite one favourable add-back.~~Repeated restructuring or maintenance costs may be part of normal operations and should not routinely be excluded.|Use evidence for each adjustment.;;Treat gains and losses consistently.;;Reconcile earnings, taxes, cash and balance-sheet consequences.
T09.17|Seasonality repeats within a year, cyclicality follows broader multi-year conditions, and base effects arise because the comparison period is unusually high or low. These mechanisms can make growth rates look stronger or weaker than current absolute activity suggests.~~Compare matching seasonal periods and inspect multiple years. A rebound after disruption is not automatically a new high-growth trajectory. Commodity and credit cycles can materially distort margins and return ratios.|Seasonality: Recurring within-year pattern.;;Cyclicality: Multi-period economic or industry fluctuations.;;Base effect: Unusual comparison denominator.|Revenue is ₹100 lakh in year one, drops to ₹50 during disruption, then recovers to ₹90.~~Year-two decline is 50%.;;Year-three growth is (90 − 50)/50 = 80%.;;Year-three revenue remains 10% below year one.~~An 80% growth headline can coexist with an incomplete recovery.~~Analyse capacity, prices and sustainable demand before extrapolating the rebound.|Compare both rates and levels.;;Use matching seasonal periods.;;Identify cycle position and abnormal bases.
T09.18|Reported performance follows the financial reporting framework. Adjusted performance applies specified exclusions or restatements. Underlying performance is an estimate of continuing economic activity and must be defined rather than assumed. These labels are not interchangeable.~~A useful reconciliation identifies each difference, its recurrence, cash effect and tax treatment. Investors should test whether the adjusted measure predicts future cash better or merely presents a more favourable picture.|Reported: Recognised financial-statement result.;;Adjusted: Explicitly reconciled alternative metric.;;Underlying: Stated estimate of ongoing activity.|Reported profit is ₹70 lakh. Management adds ₹20 share-based compensation and ₹10 restructuring to show ₹100 adjusted profit.~~Reconcile ₹70 + ₹20 + ₹10 = ₹100.;;Assess whether equity dilution makes compensation economically costly.;;Review whether restructuring recurs before excluding it from forecasts.~~The adjusted number is arithmetically reconciled but still requires economic judgment.~~A non-cash cost can transfer value or create future dilution; absence of current cash payment does not make it irrelevant.|Demand a full reconciliation.;;Assess recurrence and economic cost.;;Preserve reported figures alongside analytical adjustments.
